  "worked_solution": "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16Γ(4)}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(4)=3!=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<6)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<6) & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1}{96}\\cdot 33.86574 \\\\  & ≈0.3528\\end{aligned}\n$$",
